  "notes": "The disputed origin point of the lane, and the densest model in this batch by design — the research says dense, busy voicings of 3–5 simultaneous parts, which is `extensions` weighted 0.2/0.5/0.3 toward sevenths and ninths over a voicing register spanning 40–80, the widest here, plus a countermelody at `densityRatio` 0.65. The method is literally \"P-Funk slowed down\", so the harmony inherits the source's flat-7 vocabulary: `dorian` and `mixolydian` weighted up, and every progression family built off `VII`. ⚠ **`percs.lanes` includes `reverse`, and that is the research's fill, not decoration**: backward-masked drums are named as the fill device on these records, and `reverse` is a real lane in this engine. ⚠ The live bass is the stated core ingredient — deep, prominent, melodic funk lines with slides between chord roots, not kick-locked — so this entry carries the lowest `followRootsProb` (0.42) with the highest `glideProb` (0.7) and the widest bass register (24–48) in the batch. The whining Moog \"worm\" is the parent's `portamento_worm_lead`, inherited rather than restated, with the melody's register pushed to 98 and `octaveJumpProb` up. ⚠ `phraseBars` reaches 8 as well as 2 and 4 because the research names 4- and 8-bar loop cycles; a single length would bound every derived part. The mid-song breakdown to bass-and-drums is authored as a `bridge` rule naming only those parts, and the long intros as 8–16 bars. ⚠ The chord voicing moved up to [49, 89] so the bass stays under the harmony — it started at 40 against a bass reaching 48, an overlap of 9. The span is preserved; a voicing that low was competing with the bass rather than sitting over it.",
